docs/decisions.md:1116:| OD1 | <a id="od1"></a>References: the Austral model. A reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; loans are keyed by the root variable; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise | D2 and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (REF-5, REF-11) | exit 2i (G12), so every deliverable; bug 182's repair (exit 0b); V2 (row 6) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the Austral model: a reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; references are second-class, never stored or returned; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise; loans are keyed by the root variable (confirmed by OD15 (a), decided 2026-10-29) |
docs/decisions.md:1122:| OD7 | <a id="od7"></a>An unsafe-module marker in source (for example `trusted mod`), which never confers trust by itself; a gate lists the marked modules | D10; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509's trusted-base inventory; RocksDB slice 1 (where its `extern` declarations live); V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: an unsafe-module marker in source (for example `trusted mod`) never confers trust by itself, a gate lists the marked modules, and trust stays per method (OD22) **Revised 2026-11-03: this historical module-marker requirement was superseded by OD61 (2026-11-01), which removed `trusted mod`; trust and inventory remain per declaration.** |
docs/decisions.md:1123:| OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1128:| OD13 | <a id="od13"></a>Raw-pointer ownership: `ptr::read_owned`, an explicit replace or take through `&mut T`, a trusted ownership obligation for a raw address | T-RAWDUP and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (TRUST-7). **Provisional (bug 198):** the current compiler refuses a by-value `*p` of a non-Copy pointee through a raw pointer (E0311) and moves the value out only with the intrinsic `read_owned(p)`, with 26 std sites migrated; provisional until the owner answers | RocksDB slice 1 (its trusted binding code); R-0509, R-0514; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a value moves out of raw memory only through `read_owned(p)` or an explicit replace or take through `&mut T`, and a raw address carries a trusted ownership obligation; bug 198's provisional repair is final |
docs/decisions.md:1137:| OD22 | <a id="od22"></a>Trust on an `impl` block (added 2026-10-25, from bug 189). Bug 189's repair made the parser refuse `trusted impl` (E0001) and moved trust onto each method (`trusted fn`, 175 methods marked), while TRUST-5@1 in [the specification](../legacy/docs/spec/trusted.md#TRUST-5) still says a `trusted fn` or `trusted impl` may perform raw-pointer operations. Two options: keep the refusal and amend TRUST-5 to drop `trusted impl`, or restore `trusted impl` as TRUST-5@1 states it and revert bug 189's refusal | **Provisional (bug 189):** the current compiler refuses `trusted impl` and trusts only methods declared `trusted fn`; the specification text is unchanged, so the current compiler and TRUST-5@1 disagree; bug 189's record cites the 2026-10-23 rule that a marker never confers trust (D10, itself OD7, proposed); provisional until the owner answers | bug 189's repair (exit 0b) and its pins (`error_189_trusted_impl_refused.con`, `check_trusted_method.sh`); TRUST-5; R-0509's trusted-base inventory; OD7 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: trust is per method, `trusted fn` only; `trusted impl` stays refused, so bug 189's provisional repair is final |
docs/decisions.md:1141:| OD26 | <a id="od26"></a>TYPE-6 with TRUST-1: operators take numbers only (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | trusted and `Unsafe` code may compare raw pointers and add an integer to one; bug 322 | exit 2q; R-0502 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): arithmetic, shifts and comparisons take numbers only; pointer offset, difference and ordering are `ptr::offset` and named functions. Now TYPE-6@2 (partial, bug 322) |
docs/decisions.md:1152:| OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
docs/decisions.md:1153:| OD38 | <a id="od38"></a>The root resource API (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD8). Options: `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` with trusted splitting functions (`root.split_alloc()`, `root.split_console()`, ...) returning linear tokens; or a fixed set of token parameters `main` may declare (`fn main(alloc: Alloc, io: Console)`). Recommendation: the single `Root` value, as Austral's `RootCapability`, split by trusted functions the wrapper gate lists Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` (or Unit), `Root` split into linear tokens by trusted functions; specify the split functions and how leftover authority is consumed | `main` takes no parameters today (STMT-7) | R-0557 (TRUST-9) and STMT-7 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: there is no `Root` type; `main`'s parameters are the target's singletons, hosted `fn main() with(...) -> u8` and freestanding `fn main(uart: Uart, arena: Arena) with(Device, Alloc) -> u8`; revised 2026-11-02: `main` receives the target's grants and returns `u8` only ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
docs/decisions.md:1176:| OD61 | <a id="od61"></a>One coordinated removal list (added 2026-10-30, item review new 7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the decided and proposed removals (inline `mod`, the C-style `for`, `if let` / `while let` / `let ... else`, `newtype`, `..base`, range patterns, guards, `in R`, `break <expr>` and the `trusted mod` marker) are migrated as one coordinated list. Options: (a) one list, migrated in exit 2q's single transition; (b) each removal on its own schedule. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) one list, including dropping TRUST-6's `trusted mod` marker, which S14 removes (S14 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it). Austral: not applicable | TRUST-6 (pending) still requires the `trusted mod` marker, which S14's pick would drop (no owner answer is recorded for S14) | TRUST-6, STMT-6, PAT-6, PAT-7, PAT-4, TYPE-7, MOD-4, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One list (incl. dropping `trusted mod`) |
docs/decisions.md:1211:| OD96 | <a id="od96"></a>Two foreign bindings of one C symbol (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether two foreign bindings of one C symbol (`#[link_name]`) must agree on its C signature. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-5. The current compiler: accepted: `abs_a(x: i32) with() -> i32` and `abs_b(x: i64) with() -> i64`, both bound to `abs`, compile and the binary runs, while the interpreter stops at run time on the mismatched width. HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §5 (encoding A, one binding per effect for one C symbol) planned to refuse a mismatch with a diagnostic. Options: (a) refuse; (b) allow, each binding's signature trusted on its own. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-96 | see OPEN-96 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |
docs/decisions.md:1213:| OD98 | <a id="od98"></a>Pointer and reference conversions written with `as` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Which conversions between raw pointers and references `as` writes (`&x as *const T`, `p as &T`, `p as &mut T`, `p as *mut U`). Bears on TRUST-1, TRUST-3, REF-5, ARITH-5. The current compiler: `&x as *const T` is accepted in safe code, `p as &T` from a raw pointer is accepted in trusted or `Unsafe` code (the binary and `--interp` agree), and a reference cast between different pointee types (`a as &Int` with `a: &A`) is refused (E0553). No entry states these: TRUST-3 forms a reference from a raw pointer with `&*p`, and REF-5 lists borrowing and projection as the ways a reference is formed. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8 kept `as` "the spelling of pointer and reference casts (`as *const T`, `as *mut T`, `as &K`, `as &mut V`)", 281 lines of std. Options: (a) `as` keeps every one of them; (b) `as` converts only a reference to a raw pointer, and a reference is formed from a raw pointer only by `&*p` (TRUST-3); (c) pointer conversions become named functions. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-98 | see OPEN-98 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reference to raw pointer conversion only in trusted/Unsafe code (equal to Austral) |
docs/decisions.md:1227:| OD112 | <a id="od112"></a>Mutation through a shared reference (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value reached through a shared reference `&T` may be changed, by a write through a raw pointer derived from the reference or by a cell type. Bears on REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5. The current compiler: safe code changes a value only through a `mut` binding or a `&mut` reference, and there is no cell type; a trusted function may convert `r: &Int` to `*mut Int` (`r as *const Int as *mut Int`) and store through it: the binary does the store, and `--interp` stops ("deref-assign through immutable ref"). The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §12 said there is no `Cell`, `RefCell` or `UnsafeCell` equivalent and that mutation flows from `let mut` through `&mut`; the [overview](../legacy/docs/spec/overview.md) lists no interior mutability in safe code, with a later `UnsafeCell<T>` gated by `Unsafe`. Options: (a) never: a write through a pointer derived from a `&T` is undefined behaviour, a premise trusted code keeps (TRUST-2); (b) as (a), with a cell type gated by `Unsafe` as the one exception; (c) a trusted write through such a pointer is allowed. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | safe code cannot; a trusted write through `r as *const T as *mut T` runs in the binary and stops `--interp` | REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Never; UB premise trusted code keeps |
docs/decisions.md:1228:| OD113 | <a id="od113"></a>The ownership exceptions of trusted code (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether trusted code keeps its two exceptions to the ownership rules, destroying a value through a reference and releasing with `vec_free` a vector whose elements are not Copy. Bears on TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8. The current compiler: both are accepted in trusted code only (elsewhere E0290 under OWN-8 and E0287 under OWN-5): std's collections destroy an element through `(p as &T)` in drop glue after its owner was consumed, and a trusted function may free a buffer it has emptied. The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §11 said that `trusted` does not relax linearity, and TRUST-5's rationale says the same. Options: (a) keep both, each a named trusted operation with its premise (the owner was consumed; the vector is empty); (b) remove both, drop glue moving the element out of raw memory instead (TRUST-7); (c) keep only destruction through a reference; (d) keep only `vec_free` of an emptied vector. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted in trusted code only (E0290, E0287 elsewhere) | TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove both |
docs/decisions.md:1231:| OD116 | <a id="od116"></a>A checked total function category (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether the language has a `total fn` category, a runtime function checked to terminate without trapping that contracts may then call. Bears on CON-1, CON-3, CON-5. The current compiler: there is no such category (`total fn f(..)` is a parse error, E0001); a contract clause may still call a capability-free runtime function until CON-3's judgment lands. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Design Principles) distinguishes `spec fn`, a checked `total fn`, ordinary and trusted functions, and admits a `total fn` into contracts once its obligations close. Options: (a) add the category; (b) no category: contracts call only `spec fn` (CON-3's full judgment). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-116 | see OPEN-116 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No category |
docs/decisions.md:1233:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
docs/decisions.md:1242:| OD127 | <a id="od127"></a>UTF-8 in every `String` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether every `String` value that safe code can reach holds valid UTF-8, as a language guarantee. Bears on TYPE-3, SYN-5, TRUST-2. The current compiler: literals are UTF-8 (SYN-5), but nothing keeps a `String` valid: `push_char` appends a byte unchecked, `Bytes::to_string` transfers bytes without validation, and std's `read_to_string` does not validate. [STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md) §1 states the invariant as a target, and [UNICODE_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/UNICODE_POLICY.md) has a validating `Bytes::to_string` returning `Option`. Options: (a) a language guarantee, every safe constructor validating and an unchecked one being a trusted operation with that premise; (b) a std convention only, `String` being bytes to the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-127 | see OPEN-127 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Language guarantee |
